/// The guard that the checked-in `lib/main.reflection.dart` still describes
/// `lib/main.dart`.
///
/// The mirror is generated by `./reflection_generation.sh` and **committed**,
/// so the file in git is the one `initializeReflection()` registers and every
/// demo binds against. Nothing in Dart couples the two: adding a `@tomReflector`
/// class or a field to a state class and not regenerating leaves a tree that
/// compiles, analyzes clean, and fails only later — when a form binds a field
/// that the mirror does not know, from a place that names neither the class nor
/// this file.
///
/// ## Why a name check rather than a regeneration
///
/// The exact check is `./reflection_generation.sh --check`, which regenerates
/// and compares. It cannot live here: a run takes four to eleven minutes
/// against the whole Flutter package graph, which is far longer than this
/// suite's entire wall time and would be paid by every developer on every
/// unrelated change.
///
/// So this test buys most of that certainty for milliseconds. It cannot prove
/// the mirror is *current* — only that it is not missing the things most
/// recently added — but the failure it catches is the one that actually
/// happens: someone adds a class or a field and forgets the slow script.
///
/// ## Why names are matched as raw strings
///
/// The generator emits declaration names as raw single-quoted literals
/// (`r'CompanyProfile'`) and member names as bare identifiers, so a captured
/// class is `r'<Name>'` somewhere in the output and a captured field is its own
/// identifier. Matching the quoted form for classes is deliberate: a bare
/// `CompanyProfile` also occurs inside type references the generator emits for
/// *other* classes that mention it, so an unquoted match would report a class
/// as captured when only a reference to it survived. Fields have no such
/// quoted form, so they are matched as whole words — which can only make the
/// check weaker (a name that coincides with an unrelated identifier passes),
/// never produce a false failure.
///
/// ## What this does not check
///
/// Two things, both of which `--check` does.
///
/// Index currency: the mirror numbers every captured member in one dense global
/// sequence, so adding a single public member shifts every index after it and
/// rewrites thousands of lines. Those indices are exactly what a name check
/// cannot see.
///
/// Completeness of what *was* captured. A mirror can name every class and still
/// describe less than the source: when the generator meets a construct it
/// cannot render it leaves that piece out, writes the file anyway, and reports
/// a SEVERE diagnostic. That is how a `const` default and the two imports that
/// existed only to render it once vanished from this file with a clean analyze
/// and a green suite. The generator now prints and counts those diagnostics
/// (see `tom_reflection_generator/doc/reflection_generator.md` § Diagnostics);
/// a name check cannot substitute for reading them.
///
/// ## Why lib/ is globbed rather than listed
///
/// `build.yaml` seeds the generator from `lib/main.dart`, so the reflected
/// graph is whatever that file's imports reach — and today every annotated
/// source under `lib/` is reached. A file that is *not* reached would be
/// reported here as missing, which is the right answer: an annotated class the
/// root cannot see is not reflected, however deliberate its annotation looks.
library;

import 'dart:convert';
import 'dart:io';

import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';

/// The generated mirror, and the root it is generated from.
const String _generatedPath = 'lib/main.reflection.dart';
const String _rootPath = 'lib/main.dart';

/// Everything hand-written under `lib/`: the sources the root's import graph
/// reaches, minus the generator's own output.
Iterable<File> _handWrittenSources() => Directory('lib')
    .listSync(recursive: true)
    .whereType<File>()
    .where(
      (file) =>
          file.path.endsWith('.dart') &&
          !file.path.endsWith('.reflection.dart'),
    );

/// The classes in [source] carrying a `@tomReflector` annotation.
///
/// Attribution is line-based rather than a regex over the gap between the
/// annotation and the declaration: any character budget wide enough for a doc
/// comment is wide enough to reach past one class into the next. An annotation
/// is therefore attributed only when nothing but further annotations, comments
/// and blank lines separate it from the declaration; anything else ends the
/// run.
Set<String> _reflectorClasses(String source) {
  final found = <String>{};
  var pendingReflector = false;

  for (final raw in const LineSplitter().convert(source)) {
    final line = raw.trim();
    if (line.isEmpty || line.startsWith('//')) continue;

    final declaration = _classDeclaration.firstMatch(line);
    if (declaration != null) {
      if (pendingReflector) found.add(declaration.group(1)!);
      pendingReflector = false;
      continue;
    }

    if (line.startsWith('@')) {
      if (line == '@tomReflector') pendingReflector = true;
      continue;
    }

    pendingReflector = false;
  }
  return found;
}

/// The `late final` fields declared in [source]'s `@tomReflector` classes.
///
/// A state field is the member most often added on its own, and the one whose
/// absence from the mirror is hardest to attribute: the class still reflects,
/// so the failure is a form binding to a member that is not there rather than a
/// missing class.
///
/// The state and form classes declare their observable members uniformly as
/// `late final name = TomString('')`, which is what this reads. Fields written
/// any other way are simply not checked — under-reporting is the safe
/// direction, and the emptiness floor below is what catches the extractor
/// giving up entirely.
Set<String> _reflectorFields(String source) {
  final found = <String>{};
  var pendingReflector = false;
  var inReflectorClass = false;
  var braceDepth = 0;

  for (final raw in const LineSplitter().convert(source)) {
    final line = raw.trim();
    if (line.isEmpty || line.startsWith('//')) continue;

    final declaration = _classDeclaration.firstMatch(line);
    if (declaration != null) {
      inReflectorClass = pendingReflector;
      pendingReflector = false;
      // The declaration line usually carries the opening brace; count it so a
      // one-line class body does not leave the extractor inside a class that
      // has already closed.
      braceDepth = line.split('{').length - line.split('}').length;
      continue;
    }

    if (line.startsWith('@')) {
      if (line == '@tomReflector') pendingReflector = true;
      continue;
    }
    pendingReflector = false;

    if (!inReflectorClass) continue;

    final field = _lateFinalField.firstMatch(line);
    if (field != null) found.add(field.group(1)!);

    braceDepth += line.split('{').length - line.split('}').length;
    if (braceDepth <= 0) inReflectorClass = false;
  }
  return found;
}

/// `class Name`, with the modifiers Dart allows in front of it.
final RegExp _classDeclaration = RegExp(
  r'^(?:abstract\s+)?(?:base\s+|final\s+|interface\s+|sealed\s+)?class\s+(\w+)',
);

/// `late final name = …`, with or without an explicit type.
final RegExp _lateFinalField = RegExp(
  r'^late\s+final\s+(?:[\w<>?,\s]+\s)?(\w+)\s*=',
);

/// The remedy quoted by every failure here — one sentence, one command.
const String _regenerate =
    'The checked-in mirror is stale. Run ./reflection_generation.sh '
    '(four to eleven minutes) and commit $_generatedPath. '
    'A diff of several thousand lines is expected: one added member renumbers '
    'every index after it.';

void main() {
  final generated = File(_generatedPath).readAsStringSync();

  final declaredClasses = <String>{};
  final declaredFields = <String>{};
  for (final file in _handWrittenSources()) {
    final source = file.readAsStringSync();
    declaredClasses.addAll(_reflectorClasses(source));
    declaredFields.addAll(_reflectorFields(source));
  }

  group('$_generatedPath still describes $_rootPath', () {
    test('it captures every @tomReflector class', () {
      // Without this, an extractor that stopped matching would make the check
      // below pass while checking nothing — the failure mode a name check is
      // most prone to.
      expect(
        declaredClasses.length,
        greaterThanOrEqualTo(60),
        reason:
            'Only ${declaredClasses.length} @tomReflector class(es) were found '
            'under lib/. Either the sources moved or the annotation spelling '
            'changed, and this test is now checking almost nothing.',
      );

      final missing =
          declaredClasses
              .where((name) => !generated.contains("r'$name'"))
              .toList()
            ..sort();

      expect(
        missing,
        isEmpty,
        reason:
            '$_generatedPath does not mention the class(es) $missing.\n'
            '$_regenerate',
      );
    });

    test('it captures every reflected state field', () {
      expect(
        declaredFields.length,
        greaterThanOrEqualTo(120),
        reason:
            'Only ${declaredFields.length} reflected field(s) were found under '
            'lib/. The extractor has stopped matching the declaration form the '
            'state classes use, which weakens every field check silently.',
      );

      final missing =
          declaredFields
              .where(
                (name) =>
                    !RegExp('\\b${RegExp.escape(name)}\\b').hasMatch(generated),
              )
              .toList()
            ..sort();

      expect(
        missing,
        isEmpty,
        reason:
            '$_generatedPath does not mention the field(s) $missing.\n'
            'The class reflects but the member does not, so the failure this '
            'prevents is a form binding to a member that is not there.\n'
            '$_regenerate',
      );
    });
  });

  // The two checks are only as good as the extractors feeding them. Both
  // directions matter: an extractor that stops recognising annotations makes
  // every check pass while checking nothing, and one that over-reaches reports
  // the mirror stale for a class that was never meant to be captured.
  group('the annotation extractors read the sources as written', () {
    test('an annotation is attributed to the declaration it precedes', () {
      expect(
        _reflectorClasses('''
@tomReflector
class Reflected extends TomClass {}
'''),
        equals({'Reflected'}),
      );
    });

    test('an annotation does not reach across a class body', () {
      // The hazard a gap-based regex has: `Unreflected` follows a reflected
      // class and carries no annotation of its own.
      expect(
        _reflectorClasses('''
@tomReflector
class Reflected extends TomClass {
  late final name = TomString('');
}

class Unreflected extends TomClass {}
'''),
        equals({'Reflected'}),
      );
    });

    test('fields are read only from reflected classes', () {
      expect(
        _reflectorFields('''
@tomReflector
class Reflected extends TomClass {
  late final kept = TomString('');
}

class Unreflected extends TomClass {
  late final ignored = TomString('');
}
'''),
        equals({'kept'}),
      );
    });
  });
}
